steps.text:
[Accuracy](g:accuracy) is how close a result is to the truth. [Precision](g:precision) is how close repeat results are when nothing changed: the [typical error](g:typical-error). For an athlete tracking change, precision matters more. A method that always reads 3% fat too high still shows a real drop.

Precision comes from doing it the same way each time [[c:bc-standardise]]:

- same method, same device, same tester;
- morning, overnight-fasted, rested, bladder emptied;
- normal hydration, no hard training the day before, same clothing.

Then compare the change with the noise. As in the evidence module: the noise on a difference is √2 × the typical error, and a change needs to clear about 2 × that before you call it real.
